WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.320
In the battle against Thanos, the day was
ultimately won by a tiny, unlikely hero.

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:07.940
No, not that one, but you're close.

00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:12.880
During a spoiler filled interview with a Chinese
website, Avengers: Endgame co-director Joe

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:17.790
Russo let slip the true hero of the film,
though if you're anything like us, it had

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:22.010
probably already crossed your mind that the
Avengers victory would never have been possible

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:25.140
if not for... a random mouse.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.620
Of course, the key to the Avengers' eventual
victory was actually Scott Lang.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.730
He was last seen in the post-credits scene
for Ant-Man and the Wasp, stranded in the

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:38.730
Quantum Realm after Hank Pym, Janet Van Dyne,
and Hope Van Dyne… who were to pull him

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.210
out once he was finished collecting "healing
particles" for their new friend Ghost… were

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:45.760
all dusted before they could throw the switch
and bring Scott back.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.860
At the start of Endgame, we learn that during
the five years since the Snappening, the van

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.399
in which the Quantum Tunnel was housed had
been sitting in a rodent-infested storage.

00:00:55.399 --> 00:01:00.040
In a 14-million-to-one shot, one of those
rodents just happened to crawl across the

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:04.960
exact right spot on the Tunnel's control panel,
zapping Scott back into the world.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.280
"I went forward in time to view alternate
futures."

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:10.300
"How many did you see?"

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.520
"14 million, six hundred and five."

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:13.460
"How many did we win?"

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:15.280
"One."

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.320
This raises the question: if not for that
mouse, would Thanos have won?

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:25.570
If it would have crawled across a different
portion of the panel, even, would the Avengers

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:26.600
have been hosed?

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.479
According to Russo, the answer is yes.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.380
"The mouse saved the universe.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.560
Among the many realities in those 14 million
possible futures Doctor Strange [foresaw],

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.320
the mouse failed to press the button and thus
the heroes failed in those futures."

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.700
That's… just insane.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.780
Yes, the Avengers' plan to reverse the Decimation
was a convoluted one, with different teams

00:01:47.789 --> 00:01:51.950
traveling to different time periods in order
to pre-emptively acquire all six Infinity

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.469
Stones before Thanos could get his hands on
them.

00:01:54.469 --> 00:01:59.279
It was also fraught with complications: Tony
Stark and Steve Rogers failed to grab the

00:01:59.279 --> 00:02:04.780
Tesseract during the Battle of New York, necessitating
a slight detour to 1970; like Gamora before

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:09.829
her, Black Widow was forced to give her life
in exchange for the Soul Stone; and 2014 Thanos

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:14.819
figured out the whole plan due to Nebula's
incursion into that period, almost managing

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:19.400
to thwart our heroes by sending the still-villainous
past version of Nebula to the present as a

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:19.960
mole.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.030
But since we know that Doctor Strange saw
the only outcome that resulted in victory

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:29.879
during his future-viewing party on Titan during
Avengers: Infinity War, we know that somehow,

00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:34.500
it all played out exactly like it was supposed
to… and that included the contribution of

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.040
that low-down, dirty mouse.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:40.180
Think about what must have been going through
Doctor Strange's mind when he handed the Time

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:45.150
Stone over to Thanos, knowing that the future
of the universe depended on the random wanderings

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.000
of a mouse five years in the future.

00:02:47.269 --> 00:02:51.280
We suppose that one could take it even further,
making the case that the universe was really

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.750
saved by whatever anonymous towing company
employee decided to move Scott's van to that

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:59.620
crappy storage facility rather than a nicer,
non-vermin-infested one.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:04.099
Or the security guard who let Scott out of
the locked facility, rather than calling the

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:08.700
cops on the wild-eyed stranger who had suddenly
appeared in said facility out of nowhere.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.420
There were a lot of moving parts to the whole
thing, but in the end, we've got to go with

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:17.390
the word of Russo: a mouse was responsible
for half of all life in the universe being

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:22.129
restored, which we're sure has nothing to
do with the fact that Marvel Studios is owned

00:03:22.129 --> 00:03:22.780
by Disney.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.340
It's doubtful that Lang even knows exactly
how he was freed, which is a real shame, because

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.660
at the very least, that mouse deserves a medal.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.700
Or perhaps an honorary membership in the Avengers?

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.290
So next time you think about the Avengers
saving the universe, remember: as much as

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.620
we all love Captain America and Iron Man,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:45.340
"I only hope that we never lose sight of one
thing: that it was all started by a mouse."

